From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 09:54:30 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] uml: stop a vhost-user device whose backend has gone

vu_interrupt() warns on every result that is not a would-block errno and does
nothing else with it. When the backend closes its end of a virtqueue call
socketpair the read returns end of file, so rc is 0, so the WARN fires -- and
the descriptor stays readable, so the handler is re-entered at once, and again,
forever. One observed run collected 221534 copies of "read returned 0" and a
139 MB console before the spurious interrupt detector printed "nobody cared"
and disabled the irq, 1500 seconds after the transport had died. Nothing told
the guest the device was gone, so its userspace waited for completions that
could not arrive.

The driver already knows how to retire a dead backend: full_read() turns a read
of 0 into -ECONNRESET, vhost_user_check_reset() latches on vu_dev->registered
and schedules conn_broken_wk, and that work item breaks the virtio device and
unregisters it. Only the control socket and the backend request stream ever
reach it. Route the call and kick descriptors there too, and treat any hard
error on the request stream as a lost backend rather than only -ECONNRESET.

Stopping the interrupt source cannot wait for that work item, because the work
item cannot be scheduled out of a loop that never yields. os_del_epoll_fd() is
the one teardown step that is safe from the handler: it allocates nothing,
takes no sleeping lock, and frees no irq_entry that the interrupt loop is still
walking, which is exactly why that loop uses it to turn a descriptor off before
touching its own structures. deactivate_fd() and um_free_irq() both free the
entry the caller is walking, and um_free_irq() additionally waits on itself in
__synchronize_irq(); disable_irq_nosync() is atomic but stops neither the other
queues nor the host poll loop. Freeing the irq and closing the descriptor stay
in the device teardown, in process context.

A latch per queue and one per device keep this to a single report and let the
shared irq chain -- one irq number carries a device's request descriptor and
all of its call descriptors -- return IRQ_HANDLED for a queue that is already
gone. A would-block still returns early, so a healthy device and a spurious
wake are unaffected, and a partial notification is still a protocol WARN rather
than a dead peer.

A kick has a second shape, and it is the only one this port ever uses. In-band
notifications are in VHOST_USER_SUPPORTED_PROTOCOL_F and every backend offers
them, so vu_setup_vq() leaves kick_fd at -1 on every queue of every device and
a kick is a synchronous VHOST_USER_VRING_KICK round trip on the main socket.
That branch turned any failure of the round trip into a bare false, and
virtqueue_notify() answers a false notification by latching vq->broken --
permanently, irreversibly, with no message and no retirement. The guest is left
holding a device that can never accept another buffer and is never told. Give
the branch the same treatment as a descriptor: report once, naming the queue
and the error, stop that queue's call descriptor, and hand the device to the
connection-broken path.

Stopping the call descriptor is not optional, and the kick-descriptor path was
wrong about it too. Setting info->backend_gone makes vu_interrupt() return
without draining info->call_fd, so a completion the backend has already written
leaves that descriptor readable for ever. The read registration is level
triggered, so the handler is re-entered from the host poll loop without end and
the CPU that took the interrupt never leaves it -- measured as a vCPU pinned in
os_waiting_for_events_epoll() and an RCU stall twenty seconds later, which is
the very storm the call-descriptor path above exists to prevent. The latch and
os_del_epoll_fd() must move together, exactly as they do in vu_interrupt().

Reporting cannot cost a healthy guest its device, because that round trip has
no transient left to lose. The guest endpoint of the main socket is blocking
and neither the send nor the reply wait carries a deadline; -EINTR is retried
at every step; full_read() loops over a split header; sock_lock serializes
concurrent kicks; and backend requests that arrive during the reply wait are
drained first. A kick is acknowledged only after its queue has run, so a reply
behind a long submission is waited out rather than failed -- a reply held for
45 s, four times the host GPU watchdog's own limit, costs 45 s of wall clock
and nothing else. What is left is a desynchronized control stream, a non-zero
acknowledgement status, end of file, or a hard errno, and each of those is a
backend that is gone.

Leaving that branch silent was measured. One run broke a virtio-gpu control
queue through it and then printed 92335 WARN_ONs from drivers/gpu/drm/virtio
and 1.02 million console lines without ever stopping, because the framebuffer
console lives on that same device: each warning damages the framebuffer, whose
repair submits the two buffers that warn again. The device was never retired
and the workload never failed, it only stopped making progress.

Note that a broken virtio device is not by itself enough for virtio-gpu, which
never tests virtqueue_is_broken() and discards the -EIO from virtqueue_add_sgs();
that is a separate defect in drivers/gpu/drm/virtio.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/um/drivers/virtio_uml.c | 107 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 93 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/virtio_uml.c b/arch/um/drivers/virtio_uml.c
index a4eb1ce101ab427c58a14bc1568deb1452def089..8facd5d0bf72554b6b73bc76716222f1d338c7de 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/virtio_uml.c
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/virtio_uml.c
@@ -68,6 +68,7 @@ struct virtio_uml_device {
 	u8 suspended:1;
 	u8 no_vq_suspend:1;
 	u8 has_shmem_region:1;
+	u8 req_fd_gone:1;
 
 	/* Recorded under req_lock, taken and cleared by vu_req_interrupt() */
 	bool config_changed_irq;
@@ -83,6 +84,7 @@ struct virtio_uml_vq_info {
 	int kick_fd, call_fd;
 	char name[32];
 	bool suspended;
+	bool backend_gone;
 };
 
 #define vu_err(vu_dev, ...)	dev_err(&(vu_dev)->pdev->dev, ##__VA_ARGS__)
@@ -187,6 +189,31 @@ static void vhost_user_check_reset(struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev,
 	schedule_work(&pdata->conn_broken_wk);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Stop a descriptor whose peer has gone, then break the device.
+ *
+ * Stopping the interrupt source cannot wait for the work item that
+ * vhost_user_check_reset() schedules. The host read registration is level
+ * triggered, so a descriptor at end of file stays readable and its handler is
+ * re-entered until the spurious interrupt detector disables the irq -- and
+ * even then the host poll loop keeps spinning, because nothing has removed
+ * the descriptor from it.
+ *
+ * os_del_epoll_fd() is the one teardown step that is safe from an interrupt
+ * handler. It allocates nothing, takes no sleeping lock, and frees no
+ * irq_entry that the interrupt loop is still walking, which is why the loop
+ * itself uses it to turn a descriptor off before touching its own structures.
+ * Freeing the irq and closing the descriptor need process context and stay in
+ * the device teardown the work item reaches.
+ */
+static void vu_backend_gone(struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev, int polled_fd)
+{
+	if (polled_fd >= 0)
+		os_del_epoll_fd(polled_fd);
+
+	vhost_user_check_reset(vu_dev, -ECONNRESET);
+}
+
 #ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
 /*
  * Wait for the backend to answer on the main socket, serving its requests
@@ -650,6 +677,13 @@ static irqreturn_t vu_req_interrupt(int irq, void *data)
 	unsigned long flags;
 	uint64_t vq_map;
 
+	/*
+	 * This device's virtqueues share the irq number with the request
+	 * descriptor, so the chain still calls us after it has gone.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(vu_dev->req_fd_gone))
+		return IRQ_HANDLED;
+
 #ifdef UML_HOST_DARWIN
 	/*
 	 * Darwin has no time-travel comm-handler dispatcher.  Its request fd is
@@ -662,8 +696,12 @@ static irqreturn_t vu_req_interrupt(int irq, void *data)
 #endif
 
 	if (vu_dev->recv_rc) {
-		vhost_user_check_reset(vu_dev, vu_dev->recv_rc);
-		return ret;
+		vu_dev->req_fd_gone = 1;
+		vu_err(vu_dev,
+		       "backend request descriptor returned %d, the vhost-user backend is gone\n",
+		       vu_dev->recv_rc);
+		vu_backend_gone(vu_dev, vu_dev->req_fd);
+		return IRQ_HANDLED;
 	}
 	/*
 	 * Deliver everything recorded. A request another reader drained leaves
@@ -1064,6 +1102,7 @@ static int vhost_user_set_vring_enable(struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev,
 
 static bool vu_notify(struct virtqueue *vq)
 {
+	struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev = to_virtio_uml_device(vq->vdev);
 	struct virtio_uml_vq_info *info = vq->priv;
 	const uint64_t n = 1;
 	int rc;
@@ -1071,38 +1110,78 @@ static bool vu_notify(struct virtqueue *vq)
 	if (info->suspended)
 		return true;
 
+	if (unlikely(info->backend_gone))
+		return false;
+
 	time_travel_propagate_time();
 
 	if (info->kick_fd < 0) {
-		struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev;
-
-		vu_dev = to_virtio_uml_device(vq->vdev);
-
-		return vhost_user_set_vring_state(vu_dev, VHOST_USER_VRING_KICK,
-						  vq->index, 0) == 0;
+		/*
+		 * An in-band notification is a synchronous round trip on the
+		 * main socket. Its transient failures are already retried or
+		 * waited out inside that round trip, so anything it reports
+		 * here is a genuine transport failure.
+		 */
+		rc = vhost_user_set_vring_state(vu_dev, VHOST_USER_VRING_KICK,
+						vq->index, 0);
+		if (!rc)
+			return true;
+	} else {
+		do {
+			rc = os_write_file(info->kick_fd, &n, sizeof(n));
+		} while (rc == -EINTR);
+		if (rc == sizeof(n))
+			return true;
 	}
 
-	do {
-		rc = os_write_file(info->kick_fd, &n, sizeof(n));
-	} while (rc == -EINTR);
-	return !WARN(rc != sizeof(n), "write returned %d\n", rc);
+	info->backend_gone = true;
+	vu_err(vu_dev,
+	       "%s: kick returned %d, the vhost-user backend is gone\n",
+	       info->name, rc);
+	vu_backend_gone(vu_dev, info->call_fd);
+	return false;
 }
 
 static irqreturn_t vu_interrupt(int irq, void *opaque)
 {
 	struct virtqueue *vq = opaque;
+	struct virtio_uml_device *vu_dev = to_virtio_uml_device(vq->vdev);
 	struct virtio_uml_vq_info *info = vq->priv;
 	uint64_t n;
 	int rc;
 	irqreturn_t ret = IRQ_NONE;
 
+	/*
+	 * This device's other queues and its request descriptor share the
+	 * irq number, so the chain still calls us after this queue's call
+	 * descriptor has gone.
+	 */
+	if (unlikely(info->backend_gone))
+		return IRQ_HANDLED;
+
 	do {
 		rc = os_read_file(info->call_fd, &n, sizeof(n));
 		if (rc == sizeof(n))
 			ret |= vring_interrupt(irq, vq);
 	} while (rc == sizeof(n) || rc == -EINTR);
-	WARN(!os_error_is_would_block(rc), "read returned %d\n", rc);
-	return ret;
+
+	/* Nothing left to read is the ordinary end of a healthy pass. */
+	if (os_error_is_would_block(rc))
+		return ret;
+
+	if (rc > 0) {
+		/* A partial notification is a protocol error, not a dead peer. */
+		WARN(1, "read returned %d\n", rc);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	/* rc == 0 is end of file: the backend closed its end. */
+	info->backend_gone = true;
+	vu_err(vu_dev,
+	       "%s: call descriptor returned %d, the vhost-user backend is gone\n",
+	       info->name, rc);
+	vu_backend_gone(vu_dev, info->call_fd);
+	return IRQ_HANDLED;
 }
 
 
